---
import CaseStudyLayout from "../../layouts/CaseStudyLayout.astro";
---

<CaseStudyLayout
	title="DevHub: Maximizing Developer Productivity • Case Study"
	breadcrumbs={["VMware", "DevHub", "Lead Designer"]}
>
	<!-- ===================================================== -->
	<!-- SECTION 1: HERO & TITLE                               -->
	<!-- ===================================================== -->
	<article class="case-section">
		<!-- Eyebrow -->
		<div class="flex flex-wrap items-center justify-between gap-3 mb-4">
			<small class="eyebrow">Case Study 03 // DevHub</small>
		</div>

		<!-- Main Title -->
		<h1>Turning Fragmentation into Focus</h1>

		<!-- Lead Paragraph -->
		<div class="max-w-4xl mb-10">
			<p>
				At VMware, developers built the product, but their own
				day-to-day was scattered. Too many tools, too little clarity,
				and no single place to start.
			</p>
			<p>
				I led the design of <strong>DevHub</strong>, one place for
				20,000+ VMware engineers to find their tools, services, and
				resources, based on their role. It cut onboarding from 3 weeks
				to under 5 days.
			</p>
		</div>

		<!-- Hero Visual -->
		<figure class="m-0 mb-4">
			<div class="media-frame">
				<img
					src="/images-devhub/devhub-before-after.png"
					alt="DevHub Before and After Transformation: from fractured tools to unified command center"
					loading="eager"
				/>
			</div>
			<figcaption>
				Before and after: 20+ disconnected developer tools brought into
				one role-aware portal.
			</figcaption>
		</figure>

		<!-- Metadata Matrix Box: Role and Timeline Panel -->
		<div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-3 gap-6 mt-16">
			<div>
				<small class="kicker">role</small>
				<h4>Lead Designer</h4>
				<p>
					User research, IA, UI/UX, design system, and prototyping.
				</p>
				<small class="eyebrow"
					><time>2021</time></small
				>
			</div>
			<div>
				<small class="kicker">team &amp; scale</small>
				<h4>Core Squad &amp; Scale</h4>
				<p>
					3 Designers (including me), 8 Developers, 1 PM, for 20,000+
					engineers.
				</p>
			</div>
			<div>
				<small class="kicker">outcome</small>
				<h4>80% Faster Onboarding</h4>
				<p>
					Onboarding went from 3 weeks to under 5 days. Tool usage up
					40%. Duplicate services down 25%.
				</p>
			</div>
		</div>
	</article>

	<!-- ===================================================== -->
	<!-- SECTION 2: CONTEXT & THE CHALLENGE                    -->
	<!-- ===================================================== -->
	<article class="case-section">
		<div class="case-grid">
			<!-- Left Column -->
			<div class="case-sidebar">
				<small class="kicker">context</small>
				<h2>
					Scattered tools, low visibility, and duplicate work
				</h2>
			</div>

			<!-- Right Column -->
			<div class="case-content">
				<p>
					After years of fast growth, VMware's internal tools were
					split across departments. Engineers lost hours every week
					looking for docs, asking on Slack which tools were approved,
					and reading outdated wikis.
				</p>
				<p>
					Without a central catalog, teams often rebuilt tools that
					already existed somewhere else, and then had to maintain
					them.
				</p>

				<!-- 3 User Cohort Cards -->
				<div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-3 gap-4 my-8">
					<div class="card">
						<small class="eyebrow">DEVELOPERS</small>
						<h4>Finding vs. Coding</h4>
						<p>
							Spent more time on tribal knowledge and 80-page
							onboarding docs than on code.
						</p>
					</div>

					<div class="card">
						<small class="eyebrow">MANAGERS</small>
						<h4>Blind Spots</h4>
						<p>
							Couldn't see tool adoption or how onboarding was
							going without chasing people.
						</p>
					</div>

					<div class="card">
						<small class="eyebrow">SERVICE OWNERS</small>
						<h4>Duplication</h4>
						<p>
							Internal APIs and tools kept getting rebuilt because
							there was no way to find them across orgs.
						</p>
					</div>
				</div>

				<!-- Side by Side: Legacy Before & Pain Point Taxonomy -->
				<div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-5 pt-2">
					<figure class="m-0">
						<div class="media-frame">
							<img
								src="/images-devhub/01%20before.png"
								alt="Legacy developer portal before DevHub"
							/>
						</div>
						<figcaption>
							Before: link dumps and separate utility pages.
						</figcaption>
					</figure>

					<figure class="m-0">
						<div class="media-frame">
							<img
								src="/images-devhub/53%20pain%20points.png"
								alt="Developer pain point taxonomy and friction synthesis"
							/>
						</div>
						<figcaption>
							Pain points: too much to hold in your head, hard to
							find things, knowledge in pieces.
						</figcaption>
					</figure>
				</div>
				<figcaption class="mt-2">
					Routine tasks depended on Slack threads and personal
					bookmarks.
				</figcaption>
			</div>
		</div>
	</article>

	<!-- ===================================================== -->
	<!-- SECTION 3: RESEARCH & HEURISTIC AUDIT                 -->
	<!-- ===================================================== -->
	<article class="case-section">
		<div class="case-grid">
			<!-- Left Column -->
			<div class="case-sidebar">
				<small class="kicker">research</small>
				<h2>Interviews and a heuristic audit</h2>
			</div>

			<!-- Right Column -->
			<div class="case-content">
				<p>
					I started with <strong>12+ interviews</strong> with
					engineers, architects, and managers across business units.
					At the same time, I ran a heuristic audit of the existing
					internal portals.
				</p>

				<!-- Developer Quote Callout -->
				<blockquote>
					<p>
						"I spend more time <em>looking</em> for things than <em>building</em> things."
					</p>
					<small class="eyebrow">— MTS Developer, Cloud Infrastructure Team</small>
				</blockquote>

				<!-- Heuristic Audits Multi-Image Grid -->
				<div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-3 gap-4 pt-4">
					<figure class="m-0">
						<div class="media-frame">
							<img
								src="/images-devhub/21%20heuristics.png"
								alt="Heuristic audit on navigation clarity and mental models"
							/>
						</div>
						<figcaption>
							Audit 01. Navigation — Different in every tool.
						</figcaption>
					</figure>

					<figure class="m-0">
						<div class="media-frame">
							<img
								src="/images-devhub/22%20heuristics.png"
								alt="Heuristic audit on cognitive friction and search"
							/>
						</div>
						<figcaption>
							Audit 02. Search — No shared search across tools.
						</figcaption>
					</figure>

					<figure class="m-0">
						<div class="media-frame">
							<img
								src="/images-devhub/23%20heuristics.png"
								alt="Heuristic audit on visual hierarchy and action triggers"
							/>
						</div>
						<figcaption>
							Audit 03. Clutter — Common actions were hard to
							spot.
						</figcaption>
					</figure>
				</div>
				<figcaption class="mt-2">
					The biggest issues: recognition over recall, consistency,
					and error prevention.
				</figcaption>

				<!-- Personas and Research Highlights Grid -->
				<div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-5 pt-6">
					<figure class="m-0">
						<div class="media-frame">
							<img
								src="/images-devhub/40%20personas.png"
								alt="Developer personas: junior, senior, and lead workflows"
							/>
						</div>
						<figcaption>
							Personas, from new hires to senior architects and
							tech leads.
						</figcaption>
					</figure>

					<figure class="m-0">
						<div class="media-frame">
							<img
								src="/images-devhub/51%20research%20findings.png"
								alt="Synthesized research findings and interview insights"
							/>
						</div>
						<figcaption>
							Synthesis: hours lost each day to broken docs and
							finding tools.
						</figcaption>
					</figure>
				</div>
				<figcaption class="mt-2">
					New hires and veteran developers needed very different
					things.
				</figcaption>
			</div>
		</div>
	</article>

	<!-- ===================================================== -->
	<!-- SECTION 4: STRATEGY & ALIGNMENT                       -->
	<!-- ===================================================== -->
	<article class="case-section">
		<div class="case-grid">
			<!-- Left Column -->
			<div class="case-sidebar">
				<small class="kicker">strategy</small>
				<h2>Building it with the team, two sprints ahead</h2>
			</div>

			<!-- Right Column -->
			<div class="case-content">
				<p>
					I ran workshops with engineering and product that gave us a
					shared <strong>Experience Canvas</strong> and an <strong
						>Ecosystem Map</strong
					>. We built the backlog together, which kept design <strong
						>two sprints ahead of engineering</strong
					>.
				</p>
				<p>
					The key decision was to organize DevHub around what a
					developer is trying to do, not around the org chart:
				</p>

				<!-- Intent Pillars -->
				<div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-3 gap-4 my-8">
					<div class="card">
						<small class="eyebrow">INTENT 01</small>
						<h4>"Start a Project"</h4>
						<p>
							Toolchains, Git repos, and CI/CD pipelines set up in
							one click.
						</p>
					</div>

					<div class="card">
						<small class="eyebrow">INTENT 02</small>
						<h4>"Find a Service"</h4>
						<p>
							A service catalog with health, owners, and docs.
						</p>
					</div>

					<div class="card">
						<small class="eyebrow">INTENT 03</small>
						<h4>"Adopt Best Practices"</h4>
						<p>
							Tool recommendations by role, golden paths, and
							compliance checklists.
						</p>
					</div>
				</div>

				<!-- Full Width Ecosystem Map -->
				<figure class="m-0 pt-2">
					<div class="media-frame">
						<img
							src="/images-devhub/dh-5-workshops.avif"
							alt="DevHub developer ecosystem map connecting roles, tools, and services"
						/>
					</div>
					<figcaption>
						Ecosystem map: how personas, internal services, and cloud
						infrastructure connect.
					</figcaption>
				</figure>

				<!-- Side by Side: Experience Canvas & Strategy Backlog -->
				<div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-5 pt-6">
					<figure class="m-0">
						<div class="media-frame">
							<img
								src="/images-devhub/11%20alignment%20-%20experience%20canvas.png"
								alt="Experience canvas created during stakeholder alignment"
							/>
						</div>
						<figcaption>
							Experience Canvas: user goals, success metrics, and
							constraints.
						</figcaption>
					</figure>

					<figure class="m-0">
						<div class="media-frame">
							<img
								src="/images-devhub/dh-8-strategy.avif"
								alt="Prioritized product backlog and engineering release roadmap"
							/>
						</div>
						<figcaption>
							Roadmap: a phased rollout, with design two sprints
							ahead.
						</figcaption>
					</figure>
				</div>
			</div>
		</div>
	</article>

	<!-- ===================================================== -->
	<!-- SECTION 5: IDEATION & TACTILE PROTOTYPING             -->
	<!-- ===================================================== -->
	<article class="case-section">
		<div class="case-grid">
			<!-- Left Column -->
			<div class="case-sidebar">
				<small class="kicker">iteration</small>
				<h2>Sketching with developers</h2>
			</div>

			<!-- Right Column -->
			<div class="case-content">
				<p>
					I didn't want us to get attached to polished screens too
					early, so I ran paper sketching sessions with developers. We
					explored dashboard widgets, quick-action drawers, and
					project drill-downs.
				</p>

				<!-- 4-Grid of Sketches -->
				<div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-4 pt-2">
					<figure class="m-0">
						<div class="media-frame">
							<img
								src="/images-devhub/91%20sketch.jpg"
								alt="Paper sketch: configuring modular widgets"
							/>
						</div>
						<figcaption>
							01. Widget Configuration — Customizable cards.
						</figcaption>
					</figure>

					<figure class="m-0">
						<div class="media-frame">
							<img
								src="/images-devhub/92%20sketch.jpg"
								alt="Paper sketch: widget catalog selection"
							/>
						</div>
						<figcaption>
							02. Widget Discovery — How developers find and pin
							new widgets.
						</figcaption>
					</figure>

					<figure class="m-0">
						<div class="media-frame">
							<img
								src="/images-devhub/93%20sketch.jpg"
								alt="Paper sketch: responsive dashboard grid layout"
							/>
						</div>
						<figcaption>
							03. Dashboard Layout — Testing column densities.
						</figcaption>
					</figure>

					<figure class="m-0">
						<div class="media-frame">
							<img
								src="/images-devhub/94%20sketch.jpg"
								alt="Paper sketch: project detail drilldown"
							/>
						</div>
						<figcaption>
							04. Project Deep Dive — From overview down to a
							single repo.
						</figcaption>
					</figure>
				</div>
				<figcaption class="mt-2">
					Paper let us test ideas with engineers in the same
					session.
				</figcaption>

				<!-- Low-Fi Wireframing & Usability Testing -->
				<div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-5 pt-6">
					<figure class="m-0">
						<div class="media-frame">
							<img
								src="/images-devhub/102%20low%20fi.png"
								alt="Low-fidelity wireframe testing of command center layout"
							/>
						</div>
						<figcaption>
							Low-fi wireframes to check the hierarchy of the
							widgets.
						</figcaption>
					</figure>

					<figure class="m-0">
						<div class="media-frame">
							<img
								src="/images-devhub/112%20testing.png"
								alt="Usability testing observations and task completion logging"
							/>
						</div>
						<figcaption>
							Usability testing: timing tasks across prototype
							rounds.
						</figcaption>
					</figure>
				</div>
				<!-- Trade-off Card: Presets over blank canvas -->
				<div class="card my-8">
					<small class="eyebrow mb-2">What testing changed</small>
					<h4>Presets beat a blank canvas</h4>
					<p>
						We explored a fully configurable dashboard. In testing,
						engineers strongly preferred starting from a preset for
						their role over a blank canvas. So role-based presets
						became the default, and widgets became a way to adjust
						it.
					</p>
				</div>
			</div>
		</div>
	</article>

	<!-- ===================================================== -->
	<!-- SECTION 6: DESIGN - THE COMMAND CENTER                -->
	<!-- ===================================================== -->
	<article class="case-section">
		<div class="case-grid">
			<!-- Left Column -->
			<div class="case-sidebar">
				<small class="kicker">design</small>
				<h2>The developer command center</h2>
			</div>

			<!-- Right Column -->
			<div class="case-content">
				<p>
					The final design came down to four capabilities:
				</p>

				<!-- 4 Core Capabilities Cards -->
				<div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-5 my-8">
					<div class="card">
						<small class="eyebrow">ROLE</small>
						<h4>Role-Based Presets</h4>
						<p>
							Shows the builds, PR reviews, and tools that matter
							for your team and domain.
						</p>
					</div>

					<div class="card">
						<small class="eyebrow">SPEED</small>
						<h4>1-Click Quick Actions</h4>
						<p>
							Start a cloud sandbox, create a repo, or set up a
							pipeline in one click.
						</p>
					</div>

					<div class="card">
						<small class="eyebrow">ONBOARDING</small>
						<h4>Toolchain Bundles</h4>
						<p>
							Ready-made stacks that get new engineers to their
							first production commit within days.
						</p>
					</div>

					<div class="card">
						<small class="eyebrow">CUSTOMIZATION</small>
						<h4>Modular Widgets</h4>
						<p>
							Pin, rearrange, and configure widgets for the
							projects you're on.
						</p>
					</div>
				</div>

				<!-- Full Dashboard Hero Visual -->
				<figure class="m-0 pt-2">
					<div class="media-frame">
						<img
							src="/images-devhub/dashboard.png"
							alt="DevHub unified command center dashboard interface"
						/>
					</div>
					<figcaption>
						DevHub home: role-based tools, recent commits, CI status,
						and quick actions in one view.
					</figcaption>
				</figure>

				<!-- 4-Grid of Specific UI Innovations -->
				<div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-4 pt-6">
					<figure class="m-0">
						<div class="media-frame">
							<img
								src="/images-devhub/dh-11-quickactions.avif"
								alt="1-Click quick actions menu interface"
							/>
						</div>
						<figcaption>
							Quick actions: common tasks without leaving the
							page.
						</figcaption>
					</figure>

					<figure class="m-0">
						<div class="media-frame">
							<img
								src="/images-devhub/dh-12-onboarding.avif"
								alt="Smart onboarding toolchain interface"
							/>
						</div>
						<figcaption>
							Onboarding: a guided checklist instead of an 80-page
							manual.
						</figcaption>
					</figure>

					<figure class="m-0">
						<div class="media-frame">
							<img
								src="/images-devhub/dh-13-recommendations.avif"
								alt="Role-based recommendations and pinned resources"
							/>
						</div>
						<figcaption>
							Recommendations: tools suggested based on your
							projects.
						</figcaption>
					</figure>

					<figure class="m-0">
						<div class="media-frame">
							<img
								src="/images-devhub/131%20vd%20dashboard%20widgets.png"
								alt="Modular widget component library"
							/>
						</div>
						<figcaption>
							Widget system: other teams could build their own
							widgets.
						</figcaption>
					</figure>
				</div>
			</div>
		</div>
	</article>

	<!-- ===================================================== -->
	<!-- SECTION 7: SERVICE DISCOVERY & JOURNEYS               -->
	<!-- ===================================================== -->
	<article class="case-section">
		<div class="case-grid">
			<!-- Left Column -->
			<div class="case-sidebar">
				<small class="kicker">system</small>
				<h2>A service catalog to stop rebuilding</h2>
			</div>

			<!-- Right Column -->
			<div class="case-content">
				<p>
					To cut down duplicate services, DevHub added a <strong
						>Service Portfolio</strong
					>. Teams registered their internal APIs, libraries, and
					microservices with health, an owner, and docs.
				</p>
				<p>
					Now, before building something new, engineers could check
					whether it already existed and reuse it.
				</p>

				<!-- 3-Step Service Portfolio Exploration -->
				<div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-3 gap-4 pt-4">
					<figure class="m-0">
						<div class="media-frame">
							<img
								src="/images-devhub/Day%201%20-%20Service%20Portfolio%20Home.png"
								alt="Service portfolio home catalog"
							/>
						</div>
						<figcaption>
							01. Catalog — Search internal services by status.
						</figcaption>
					</figure>

					<figure class="m-0">
						<div class="media-frame">
							<img
								src="/images-devhub/Day%201%20-%20Service%201.1.png"
								alt="Service detail page showing health, documentation, and dependencies"
							/>
						</div>
						<figcaption>
							02. Service Detail — Health, endpoints, and who
							owns it.
						</figcaption>
					</figure>

					<figure class="m-0">
						<div class="media-frame">
							<img
								src="/images-devhub/Day%201%20-%20Service%20Details%201.1.png"
								alt="Service configuration and integration details"
							/>
						</div>
						<figcaption>
							03. Integration — Code snippets and client
							libraries.
						</figcaption>
					</figure>
				</div>
				<figcaption class="mt-2">
					One catalog for the whole org, instead of services hidden
					in team repos.
				</figcaption>

				<!-- Proposed User Journey Mapping Visual -->
				<div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-5 pt-6">
					<figure class="m-0">
						<div class="media-frame">
							<img
								src="/images-devhub/81%20proposed%20user%20journey.png"
								alt="Proposed developer onboarding user journey"
							/>
						</div>
						<figcaption>
							Journey 01: Day 1 to first production commit.
						</figcaption>
					</figure>

					<figure class="m-0">
						<div class="media-frame">
							<img
								src="/images-devhub/82%20proposed%20user%20journey.png"
								alt="Proposed continuous development user journey"
							/>
						</div>
						<figcaption>
							Journey 02: A normal day of triage, builds, and
							reviews.
						</figcaption>
					</figure>
				</div>
			</div>
		</div>
	</article>

	<!-- ===================================================== -->
	<!-- SECTION 8: ACCESSIBILITY & DESIGN GOVERNANCE          -->
	<!-- ===================================================== -->
	<article class="case-section">
		<div class="case-grid">
			<!-- Left Column -->
			<div class="case-sidebar">
				<small class="kicker">governance</small>
				<h2>Handoff and accessibility</h2>
			</div>

			<!-- Right Column -->
			<div class="case-content">
				<p>
					A tool for 20,000+ engineers had to work for everyone. I
					wrote the accessibility specs for keyboard navigation,
					screen reader focus order, and heading structure.
				</p>

				<!-- Handoff Specs Multi-Grid -->
				<div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-3 gap-4 pt-4">
					<figure class="m-0">
						<div class="media-frame">
							<img
								src="/images-devhub/141%20-%20dev%20handoff%20-%20ally%20buttons.png"
								alt="Accessibility specifications for button components"
							/>
						</div>
						<figcaption>
							A11y Specs 01 — Target sizes, contrast, and
							states.
						</figcaption>
					</figure>

					<figure class="m-0">
						<div class="media-frame">
							<img
								src="/images-devhub/141%20-%20dev%20handoff%20-%20ally%20focus%20order.png"
								alt="Keyboard navigation and focus order specification"
							/>
						</div>
						<figcaption>
							A11y Specs 02 — Tab order for keyboard users.
						</figcaption>
					</figure>

					<figure class="m-0">
						<div class="media-frame">
							<img
								src="/images-devhub/141%20-%20dev%20handoff%20-%20ally%20headings.png"
								alt="Heading hierarchy and semantic landmark mapping"
							/>
						</div>
						<figcaption>
